<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Guiengola</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Guiengola is a fortified Zapotec hilltop site near Tehuantepec in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oaxaca">Oaxaca</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, with temples, palaces, a ball court and long defensive walls. In the late 1490s the Zapotec king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cocijoeza">Cocijoeza</a> withstood a long siege there by the armies of the Aztec ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahuitzotl">Ahuitzotl</a>. The war ended in a negotiated peace, traditionally sealed by Cocijoeza's marriage to an Aztec princess.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/guiengola.jpg" alt="Guiengola" width="320" height="200" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: HJPD · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Guiengola3.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
